I searched specifically for systems we can steal mechanics and architecture from. There are some **very strong precedents**, but I didn't find anything that combines the full RoboBladez loop: deterministic physics + sealed autonomous strategies + persistent evolving AI characters + league history + post-hoc cinematic series.

The closest existing build is **Robocode Tank Royale**. It is almost a stripped-down ancestor of RoboBladez: programmers submit autonomous bots, humans have no direct control during battle, rules constrain what bots can do, gameplay is deterministic, and the system supports network battles. Classic Robocode also has a control API for launching matches, extracting battle results and per-turn snapshots, plus recorded-battle replay. ([Robocode][1])

That repo/system is probably the **#1 thing we should study**.

## 1. Robocode: steal the separation of engine and competitor

Robocode's philosophy is basically:

```text
FIXED GAME ENGINE

        ↑ API ↓

UNTRUSTED COMPETITOR CODE
```

The competitor gets a deliberately limited API. It can observe certain things and issue legal actions, but it cannot alter the game rules. ([Robocode][2])

That is exactly what RoboBladez needs.

Not:

```text
"submit arbitrary Python"
```

but:

```text
RoboBlade Policy API

observe()
move()
spin()
brake()
activate_ability()
```

with CPU/time/memory limits.

The engine owns:

```text
physics
collision
energy
arena
time
legality
win conditions
```

This solves a huge amount of the old architecture.

