# 8. The game theory gets REALLY interesting: PSRO

This might be the research concept I'd import most strongly.

**Policy-Space Response Oracles (PSRO)** essentially maintains a population of strategies.

Conceptually:

```text
Strategy A
Strategy B
Strategy C

↓ play against one another

payoff matrix

       A    B    C
A      -   .7   .2
B     .3    -   .8
C     .8   .2    -
```

Then generate new strategies that are good responses to the existing population.

OpenSpiel already implements PSRO. ([GitHub][12]) Research on PSRO treats this as a scalable method for studying huge strategic spaces by maintaining useful populations of policies rather than trying to represent every possible strategy. ([arXiv][13])

This is almost **RoboBlade evolution mathematically formalized**.

Instead of:

```text
Boris gets "smarter"
```

we can retain:

```text
Boris-v1
Boris-v2
Boris-v3
Boris-v4
```

and ask:

```text
which historical policy counters which?
```

Maybe v7 beats v9.

That's interesting.

Evolution doesn't have to be strictly:

```text
newer = stronger
```

