# 9. This gives us rock-paper-scissors metas

And there is a fantastic piece of game-theory research here with an almost comically appropriate title:

## **Real World Games Look Like Spinning Tops**

DeepMind researchers studied games including Go and StarCraft II and found that game strategy populations often contain both:

* **transitive strength**: A is broadly stronger than B;
* **non-transitive cycles**: A beats B, B beats C, C beats A.

They represented that strategy geometry as a **spinning top**. ([arXiv][14])

For RoboBladez this would be phenomenal.

Instead of:

```text
#1 blade
#2 blade
#3 blade
```

you might discover:

```text
BORIS
beats
MORTY

MORTY
beats
ALICE

ALICE
beats
BORIS
```

Because their strategies counter one another.

Now the audience can't just say:

> Boris has 92 power therefore Boris wins.

The **matchup matters**.

That is much more like a real competitive sport.

