# 11. Actual Beyblade physics is less solved than you'd think

I found a genuinely relevant 2021 research project specifically motivated by Beyblades moving inside a spherical stadium.

It modeled a spinning top using:

```text
gravity
normal force
friction
rolling resistance
air resistance
```

plus associated torques.

The author built a C# simulation and reported qualitatively Beyblade-like motion, but found meaningful disagreement with real experimental results, with rolling resistance suspected as one incomplete part of the model. ([Society for Science][15])

This is useful because it tells us:

**don't assume default rigid-body PyBullet settings magically equal accurate Beyblade physics.**

